4. Now compare two branches using git diff command. The basic syntax is: 
   ```
   git diff <branch1> <branch2>
   ```
   Replace `<branch1>` and `<branch2>` with names of the branches you want to compare. For example, if you're comparing `branch1` and `branch2`, the command would be: 
   ```
   git diff branch1 branch2
   ```
5. Press enter in your terminal. Git will then display a list of all changes made between these two branches. Each change is prefixed with "+" for added lines or "-" for removed lines. If a line has been modified, it's shown as `-` and `+` on both sides.
6. You can also use the `--stat` option to get a summary of changes:
   ```
   git diff --stat branch1 branch2
   ```
7. To see the actual differences in context (i.e., with surrounding lines), you can use the `-p` or `--patch` option:
   ```
   git diff -p branch1 branch2
   ```
8. If you want to compare a specific file between two branches, specify the filename after the command:
   ```
   git diff branch1 branch2 -- path/to/file
   ```
9. You can also use `git log` with `--follow` option to see who last modified a particular file and when:
   ```
   git log --follow -p <file>
   ```
Remember, the changes are shown from `branch1` to `branch2`. If you want to see what's in `branch2` that is not in `branch1` (i.e., it has been merged into branch1), use:
```
git diff branch1..branch2
```
This command uses the double dot notation, which shows all commits reachable from `branch2` but not from `branch1`. 

I hope this helps you understand how to use git diff effectively for comparing branches or commits in your repository. If you have any more questions, feel free to ask.

