```python
import difflib

def semantic_similarity(patch1, patch2):
    lines1 = patch1.split('\n')[3:] # Skip the first three lines which are usually metadata
    lines2 = patch2.split('\n')[3:]
    
    matcher = difflib.SequenceMatcher(None, lines1, lines2)
    similar_lines = [match for match in matcher.get_matching_blocks() if match.size > 0]
    
    print("Similar Lines:")
    for line in similar_lines:
        print(f"Patch 1 Line {line.a+1} to Patch 2 Line {line.b+1}: '{lines1[line.a]}' and '{lines2[line.b]}'")
    
    if len(similar_lines) > 0:
        print("Are these two patches semantically similar? (yes/no): ")
    else:
        print("The two patches are not semantically similar.")
```

This Python function takes in the contents of two patch files as strings, compares them line by line using difflib's SequenceMatcher and prints out which lines from each file are most similar. It then asks if these two patches are semantically similar. 

Please note that this is a very basic semantic similarity check and might not be accurate for complex code changes. For more advanced semantic analysis, you may need to use machine learning models trained on large codebases or libraries like SpaCy.

