### Response:
In software development, semantic similarity refers to how closely related two pieces of code are semantically. This concept is similar to what we mean by "semantic similarity" in linguistics. 

Let's take an example with Java classes and methods. Consider the following pair of classes:

```java
public class Employee {
    private String name;
    
    public void setName(String newName) {
        this.name = newName;
    }
}

public class Manager extends Employee {
    private List<Employee> subordinates;
    
    public void addSubordinate(Employee employee) {
        this.subordinates.add(employee);
    }
}
```
In the above example, `Manager` and `Employee` are semantically similar because they both represent different types of entities (a manager is a type of employee). They have methods that perform operations on these entities - setting their names and adding subordinates. 

