public class Employee {
    private String name;
    
    public void setName(String newName) {
        this.name = newName;
    }
}

public class Person {
    private String name;
    
    public void rename(String newName) {
        this.name = newName;
    }
}
```
In the second pair of classes, `Employee` and `Person` are not semantically similar because they represent different types of entities (an employee is a type of person). They have methods that perform operations on these entities - setting their names. 

So in software development, semantic similarity refers to how closely related two pieces of code or components are semantically. It helps developers understand the purpose and functionality of each piece of code more effectively by providing context about what it does. This understanding can help them write better code, debug issues, and maintain their systems.

