A: You can use the findall() method in combination with the tag name to extract specific data from your XML file. Here's how you could modify your script to get all 'name' elements that are children of a 'person' element:

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
tree = ET.parse('file.xml')
root = tree.getroot()
for person in root.findall('.//person'):  # find all 'person' elements
    name_elements = person.findall('name')  # find all 'name' children of the current 'person' element
    for name in name_elements:
        print(name.text)  # prints out the text content of each 'name' element
```
In this script, `root.findall('.//person')` finds all 'person' elements anywhere in the XML file (the "." means match any tag). For each 'person', it then finds its child 'name' elements with `person.findall('name')` and prints out their text content with `print(name.text)`.

Please note that if your XML data contains namespaces, you will need to use the appropriate namespace prefixes in your XPath expressions. The above code assumes no namespaces. If there are any, they would have to be handled separately. 

Also, remember that `findall()` returns a list of elements, so even if there's only one match, it will still return a list with one element. This is why we iterate over the result in the second loop.

