from sklearn.metrics import pairwise_distances
import numpy as np

def semantic_similarity(patch1, patch2):
    # Split the patches into lines
    lines_patch1 = patch1.split('\n')
    lines_patch2 = patch2.split('\n')
    
    # Calculate pairwise distances between line vectors using cosine distance
    dists = pairwise_distances(lines_patch1, lines_patch2, metric='cosine')
    
    # Find the most similar lines in each patch
    min_idx_p1 = np.argmin(dists, axis=1)
    min_idx_p2 = np.argmin(dists, axis=0)
    
    # Report which lines are more similar
    print("Patch 1 most similar lines:")
    for i in range(len(lines_patch1)):
        if i == min_idx_p2[min_idx_p1[i]]:
            print(f"Line {i+1} in Patch 1 is more similar to Line {min_idx_p1[i]+1} in Patch 2")
    
    print("\nPatch 2 most similar lines:")
    for i in range(len(lines_patch2)):
        if i == min_idx_p1[min_idx_p2[i]]:
            print(f"Line {i+1} in Patch 2 is more similar to Line {min_idx_p2[i]+1} in Patch 1")
    
    # Determine if the patches are semantically similar based on a threshold
    threshold = 0.35  # You can adjust this threshold as needed
    avg_dist = np.mean(dists)
    print("\nSemantic similarity: ", "Yes" if avg_dist <= threshold else "No")
```

This function first splits the patches into lines, then calculates pairwise distances between line vectors using cosine distance from scikit-learn's metrics module. It finds the most similar lines in each patch by finding the minimum distances along each row (for Patch 1) and column (for Patch 2). The function reports which lines are more similar to each other, then determines if the patches are semantically similar based on an average distance threshold.

