## Instruction:

I am trying to understand how to use the `git diff` command to compare files in different commits. How can I do this?

## Response:

You can use the `git diff` command with two commit hashes (or tags) to see the differences between those two points in time. 

Here's a basic usage of git diff:

```bash
git diff <commit1> <commit2>
```

Replace `<commit1>` and `<commit2>` with your actual commit hashes or tags. This will show you the differences between those two points in time. 

If you want to see changes line by line, use the -p (patch) option:

```bash
git diff -p <commit1> <commit2>
```

This is especially useful if you're trying to understand a complex commit that includes multiple files and/or numerous lines of code. 

If you want to see changes in context, use the -c (context) option:

```bash
git diff -c <commit1> <commit2>
```

This will show you three lines of "context" around each change. This can be helpful if you're trying to understand a complex commit that includes multiple files and/or numerous lines of code. 

Remember, the `<commit1>` should come before `<commit2>` in most cases as git diff compares two points in time. If you switch them around, it will show changes from `<commit2>` to `<commit1>` instead.

