```python
def semantic_similarity(patch1: str, patch2: str) -> bool:
    # Split patches into lines
    patch1_lines = patch1.split('\n')[3:-4]  # Skip the first and last few lines that are usually metadata
    patch2_lines = patch2.split('\n')[3:-4]  # Same as above
    
    # Check if any line in patch1 is more semantically similar to a line in patch2
    for line1 in patch1_lines:
        for line2 in patch2_lines:
            if line1.strip() == line2.strip():
                return True
    
    return False
```
This function compares each line of one patch with every line of the other patch, and returns `True` as soon as it finds a pair of lines that are semantically similar (i.e., they have identical content after stripping leading/trailing whitespace). If no such pair is found in either patch, it returns `False`.

