In both cases, we're counting from zero to nine. Yet, they are different pieces of code because the second one includes more detailed steps. But remember, even though they have different routes, they still end up at their destination - counting numbers 0 through 9. This is a concept known as semantic similarity in programming. It refers to how similar two pieces of code or programs are semantically, meaning they accomplish the same task but may use different methods or syntax.

